  note = {Insights: Introduces a task-oriented, multi-stage trust evaluation framework that leverages generative AI to reason over asynchronous, incomplete device attribute data, enabling accurate collaborator selection in distributed and resource-constrained systems. \textbar\textbar{} TL;DR: This paper proposes Chain-of-Trust, a progressive trust evaluation framework that decomposes trust assessment into task-aligned stages and uses generative AI with few-shot chain-of-thought reasoning to achieve high accuracy with reduced overhead. \textbar\textbar{} Contributions: - Introduces the Chain-of-Trust framework, which decomposes trust evaluation into sequential, task-driven stages (task decomposition, service availability, communication resources, computing resources, result delivery). - Demonstrates how generative AI enables few-shot, chain-of-thought reasoning for trust assessment without task-specific training. - Addresses asynchronous and misaligned device attribute data by collecting only stage-relevant attributes at each step. - Provides an experimental evaluation across heterogeneous devices showing significant accuracy gains over standard LLM and chain-of-thought baselines. - Reframes trust as task-specific and multi-dimensional rather than synonymous with security. \textbar\textbar{} Challenges: - Centralized server assumption may limit applicability in fully decentralized environments. - Evaluation relies on simulated collaborative systems rather than large-scale real-world deployments. - Performance depends on the reasoning capabilities of frontier LLMs. - Trust metrics beyond accuracy (e.g., robustness, adversarial resistance) require further study.},
